declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 19, $j$5.3 First and Second Peter: Hope in Suffering$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "1 Peter 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/1Peter/1"}, {"title": "1 Peter 2 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/1Peter/2"}, {"title": "1 Peter 5 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/1Peter/5"}, {"title": "2 Peter 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/2Peter/3"}], "body": "Read 1 Peter 1, 1 Peter 2, 1 Peter 5 and 2 Peter 3 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nThe apostle Peter wrote two letters near the end of his life. First Peter was written to believers scattered across Asia Minor who were suffering for their faith. Peter encouraged them with a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ. He taught that the trial of their faith is more precious than gold, that they are a chosen generation and a royal priesthood, and that Christ left them an example to follow. In chapter 5 he told them to humble themselves, to cast all their care on God because He cares for them, and to be sober and watchful against the devil. Second Peter was written to warn about false teachers and to remind believers that the Lord is patient and wants all to come to repentance. Peter ended by urging them to grow in grace and in the knowledge of Jesus Christ.\n\nKey words: living hope, trial, royal priesthood, care, grow.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell why Peter wrote his first letter. (2) Write 1 Peter 5:7 in your own words. (3) Explain what Peter meant when he said the Lord is longsuffering in 2 Peter 3.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "The apostle Peter wrote the next two letters. You will remember Peter from the Gospels and Acts. He was the fisherman who denied Jesus three times, was forgiven and restored, preached at Pentecost, and became a leader of the early church. Near the end of his life he wrote letters to strengthen other believers.\n\nFirst Peter was written to Christians scattered across Asia Minor who were suffering for their faith. Peter began with praise. First Peter 1:3 says, Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, which according to his abundant mercy hath begotten us again unto a lively hope by the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead, A lively hope means a living hope. It is alive because Jesus is alive. Peter then explained that trials have a purpose. First Peter 1:7 says, That the trial of your faith, being much more precious than of gold that perisheth, though it be tried with fire, might be found unto praise and honour and glory at the appearing of Jesus Christ: Gold is refined in fire, and our faith is made stronger in trials.\n\nPeter also called believers to live holy lives. First Peter 1:16 says, Because it is written, Be ye holy; for I am holy. And in chapter 2 he reminded them who they are. First Peter 2:9 says, But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light: Peter pointed to Jesus as our example in verse 21, For even hereunto were ye called: because Christ also suffered for us, leaving us an example, that ye should follow his steps:\n\nIn chapter 5 Peter gave advice that every student needs. First Peter 5:6 says, Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time: And verse 7 says, Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you. We do not have to carry our worries alone. Then verse 8 warns, Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour: We must stay alert.\n\nSecond Peter was written shortly before Peter died. False teachers were appearing in the church, and some people were mocking the promise of the Lord's return. Peter answered in 2 Peter 3:8, But, beloved, be not ignorant of this one thing, that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day. God is not slow. He is patient, because He wants everyone to have time to repent. Peter closed with these words in 2 Peter 3:18, But grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. To him be glory both now and for ever. Amen.\n\nYour task is to tell why Peter wrote his first letter, write 1 Peter 5:7 in your own words, and explain what Peter meant when he said the Lord is patient. Then take the four question check.\n\nCast your care on Him, and keep growing. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$5.3 First and Second Peter: Hope in Suffering$j$);
end
$seed$;
